From: Mykola Lysenko <nickolay.lysenko@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] btrfs: track file extents on NO_HOLES filesystems to avoid null files after crash

The per-inode file extent tree exists so that
btrfs_inode_safe_disk_i_size_write() only advances disk_i_size over
ranges whose extents (or hole records) have actually been persisted,
preventing a crash from exposing a file whose committed i_size outruns
its committed data ("null files": non-zero size, no extents).

btrfs_init_file_extent_tree() skips allocating the tree when the
filesystem has NO_HOLES, on the grounds that an extentless tail is
representable there, so the resulting metadata is valid.  Valid,
however, is not the same as expected: with the tree absent,
btrfs_inode_safe_disk_i_size_write() falls back to setting
disk_i_size = i_size unconditionally, so the sequence

	pwrite (buffered) -> truncate(same size) -> transaction commit
	-> crash before delalloc writeback

persists size=N nbytes=0 with zero EXTENT_DATA items.  After log
replay the file reads back as N bytes of zeroes.  fsck is clean (the
tail is a legal implicit hole), so nothing flags the data loss.

This is precisely the class generic/044-046 guard against, and they
fail deterministically on any NO_HOLES filesystem (the mkfs default
since btrfs-progs 5.15) when the commit window lands between the
truncate and writeback: mkfs defaults + mount -o commit=1 fails
generic/044 15/15 on x86-64 KVM.  A single-variable A/B (mkfs
-O ^no-holes, same kernel, same load) goes 0/8.

Allocate the file extent tree on NO_HOLES filesystems too.  Every
marking path already handles NO_HOLES correctly: hole filling marks
implicit-hole ranges in the tree even when it elides the hole item
(btrfs_cont_expand()), ordered extent completion, truncation and log
replay mark their ranges unconditionally.  Only the allocation was
skipped, which turns btrfs_inode_safe_disk_i_size_write() into a no-op
protection.  The cost is one extent_io_tree per in-memory regular
inode, the same as every non-NO_HOLES filesystem already pays.

Three paths deliberately create implicit holes on NO_HOLES and must
record them in the tree, since an implicit hole is committed state the
moment its transaction commits: fill_holes() (hole punching and extent
replacement below i_size), clone_finish_inode_update() (a clone
extending i_size over a cloned-in hole, e.g. reflink-copying a sparse
file), and log replay below.  Without these, the disk_i_size clamp
persists a size below the data (observed live, no crash needed: cp of
a fully-sparse file came back size 0 after remount; fstests
generic/303, generic/547, generic/574 and btrfs/332 catch variants).

Log replay needs one adjustment: on NO_HOLES filesystems the log
carries no hole items, so replaying only the logged extents would leave
implicit-hole gaps unmarked and a later disk_i_size update would clamp
the file below its replayed size.  During replay the on-disk state is
fully committed — a gap below a replayed extent is a true hole — so
mark the range cumulatively from offset zero.

Also drop the assertion in btrfs_find_contiguous_extent_bit() and the
comment in btrfs_inode_safe_disk_i_size_write() that encoded the
tree-less NO_HOLES arrangement.

Reproducible with fstests generic/044, generic/045, generic/046 on
default mkfs; verified on both x86-64/KVM and UML, and at current
for-next (374fd5d128d8).

Signed-off-by: Mykola Lysenko <nickolay.lysenko@gmail.com>
---
--- a/fs/btrfs/inode.c
+++ b/fs/btrfs/inode.c
@@ -3996,8 +3996,6 @@ static int btrfs_init_file_extent_tree(struct btrfs_inode *inode)

 	if (WARN_ON_ONCE(inode->file_extent_tree))
 		return 0;
-	if (btrfs_fs_incompat(fs_info, NO_HOLES))
-		return 0;
 	if (!S_ISREG(inode->vfs_inode.i_mode))
 		return 0;
 	if (btrfs_is_free_space_inode(inode))
--- a/fs/btrfs/reflink.c
+++ b/fs/btrfs/reflink.c
@@ -40,6 +40,27 @@
 	if (endoff > destoff + olen)
 		endoff = destoff + olen;
 	if (endoff > vfs_inode->i_size) {
+		/*
+		 * On NO_HOLES filesystems the clone may have created an
+		 * implicit hole (no items) in the extended range — e.g. when
+		 * the source range is itself a hole.  That hole becomes
+		 * committed state with this transaction, so record it in the
+		 * file extent tree or the disk_i_size clamp below would
+		 * persist a size under @endoff.
+		 */
+		if (btrfs_fs_incompat(inode->root->fs_info, NO_HOLES)) {
+			int ret2;
+
+			ret2 = btrfs_inode_set_file_extent_range(inode,
+					vfs_inode->i_size,
+					round_up(endoff, inode->root->fs_info->sectorsize) -
+					vfs_inode->i_size);
+			if (ret2) {
+				btrfs_abort_transaction(trans, ret2);
+				btrfs_end_transaction(trans);
+				return ret2;
+			}
+		}
 		i_size_write(vfs_inode, endoff);
 		btrfs_inode_safe_disk_i_size_write(inode, 0);
 	}
--- a/fs/btrfs/file.c
+++ b/fs/btrfs/file.c
@@ -2051,8 +2051,19 @@
 	u64 num_bytes = 0;
 	int ret;
 
-	if (btrfs_fs_incompat(fs_info, NO_HOLES))
+	if (btrfs_fs_incompat(fs_info, NO_HOLES)) {
+		/*
+		 * No hole item to insert, but the implicit hole this range
+		 * becomes is committed state: record it in the file extent
+		 * tree so btrfs_inode_safe_disk_i_size_write() can advance
+		 * disk_i_size over it.
+		 */
+		ret = btrfs_inode_set_file_extent_range(inode, offset,
+							end - offset);
+		if (ret)
+			return ret;
 		goto out;
+	}
 
 	key.objectid = btrfs_ino(inode);
 	key.type = BTRFS_EXTENT_DATA_KEY;
--- a/fs/btrfs/tree-log.c
+++ b/fs/btrfs/tree-log.c
@@ -991,7 +991,17 @@
 		goto out;
 
 update_inode:
-	ret = btrfs_inode_set_file_extent_range(inode, start, extent_end - start);
+	/*
+	 * On NO_HOLES filesystems the log carries no hole items, so gaps
+	 * below this extent would stay unmarked and a later
+	 * btrfs_inode_safe_disk_i_size_write() would clamp i_size under the
+	 * replayed size.  Everything below a replayed extent is committed
+	 * on-disk state (data or implicit hole), so mark from offset zero.
+	 */
+	if (btrfs_fs_incompat(root->fs_info, NO_HOLES))
+		ret = btrfs_inode_set_file_extent_range(inode, 0, extent_end);
+	else
+		ret = btrfs_inode_set_file_extent_range(inode, start, extent_end - start);
 	if (ret) {
 		btrfs_abort_log_replay(wc, ret,
 	       "failed to set file extent range [%llu, %llu) inode %llu root %llu",
--- a/fs/btrfs/extent-io-tree.c
+++ b/fs/btrfs/extent-io-tree.c
@@ -1068,8 +1068,6 @@ bool btrfs_find_contiguous_extent_bit(struct extent_io_tree *tree, u64 start,
 	struct extent_state *state;
 	bool ret = false;

-	ASSERT(!btrfs_fs_incompat(btrfs_extent_io_tree_to_fs_info(tree), NO_HOLES));
-
 	spin_lock(&tree->lock);
 	state = find_first_extent_bit_state(tree, start, bits);
 	if (state) {
--- a/fs/btrfs/file-item.c
+++ b/fs/btrfs/file-item.c
@@ -31,13 +31,9 @@
  * @inode:      inode we want to update the disk_i_size for
  * @new_i_size: i_size we want to set to, 0 if we use i_size
  *
- * With NO_HOLES set this simply sets the disk_is_size to whatever i_size_read()
- * returns as it is perfectly fine with a file that has holes without hole file
- * extent items.
- *
- * However without NO_HOLES we need to only return the area that is contiguous
- * from the 0 offset of the file.  Otherwise we could end up adjust i_size up
- * to an extent that has a gap in between.
+ * Only advance disk_i_size over the area that is contiguous from offset 0
+ * of the file according to the file extent tree.  Otherwise we could end up
+ * adjusting i_size up over data that was never persisted.
  *
  * Finally new_i_size should only be set in the case of truncate where we're not
  * ready to use i_size_read() as the limiter yet.
